WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Okay, I have been experimenting a lot

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.840
with AI videos lately and the results

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.400
have been truly

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.520
mind-blowing. Like it is crazy how

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.520
realistic these videos have gotten and

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.640
what is even crazier is that if you have

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.480
the right AI tool workflow, then you can

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.040
make insanely realistic videos just like

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.680
these in less than 30 minutes. Like I

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:25.360
saw some viral AI dog podcast videos on

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.040
Facebook, so I tried making it myself

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.840
and I can now make these little mini

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:33.400
episodes in just 15 minutes. Check it

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:37.680
out. So, here is a question. Who has it

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:40.680
harder, dogs or humans? Dogs, definitely

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.960
dogs. Yeah, I I I agree. I don't think

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.520
most humans realize

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.880
how easy they have it.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.880
>> Yeah, if humans want to eat a treat,

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.960
they just go to the kitchen and eat it.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.400
No one makes them do a trick to earn it

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.640
first, you know?

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.440
>> Yeah, I bet if you made a human do a sit

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.880
before you let them eat treats, they

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.200
would lose their minds. See, isn't it

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.400
amazing? So, here is the workflow that I

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.480
use to make these videos. My workflow

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.560
uses three AI tools, Google's image

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.480
generator Nano Banana, their video

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.000
generator Veo, and a lesser-known but

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.040
still very important tool, Flow. And

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.440
it's kind of funny because honestly,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.560
very few people even know that Flow

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.280
exists, which honestly makes sense

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.520
because I think that Google has done a

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.480
pretty poor job of advertising it, but

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.400
basically it is their all-in-one image

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.600
and video generation tool designed

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.200
specifically for video creators that

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.480
they have recently given a huge new

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.080
update to. And it's pretty simple to use

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.680
once you figure out how to find it. So,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.240
once logged in, you can just click to

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.960
create a new project and then in here,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.080
you can either generate pictures using

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.760
Nano Banana or you can switch to

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.640
Google's video generator Veo to generate

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.800
videos. And there are two simple ways

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.720
that you can access Flow. The official

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.600
way is that you're supposed to access it

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.240
through Google's AI Lab website

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.400
labs.google.com.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.720
This website contains a directory of all

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.760
of their experimental AI tools. So, if

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.960
you browse through their different

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:09.039
creation tools, you'll be able to find

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:11.400
it in here. So, this is one way. Or

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.720
alternatively, an even simpler way is to

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.120
just do a search for it in Google and

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:18.480
boom, you'll find it. Now, to actually

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.480
use this, you do need a paid Google AI

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.320
subscription. And so, if you don't have

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.200
one, I recommend that you scroll down

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.280
the Google Flow homepage and when you

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.960
do, you will come to pricing and you

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.400
will see that Google are still giving

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.840
away a one-month free subscription to

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.040
their pro plan for all new accounts. It

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.800
is a crazy good deal. So, if they're

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.080
still offering it, I highly recommend

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.000
signing up for it and accessing it for

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.800
free. And then once you're inside, we

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.640
can generate our starting video frame

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.480
with Google's image generator Nano

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.680
Banana. Yes, because you see, here's the

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.560
thing, right? Almost all AI videos are

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.760
made these days by first creating a

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.920
still image of the starting frame and

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.920
then turning the image into a video

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.840
because this almost always creates the

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.080
best result. So, we'll be doing that

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.200
too. And to do that inside of Flow, just

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.120
click to create a new project. Then come

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.880
to the chat menu and select to create an

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.520
image. You can choose between either

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.320
landscape image or a portrait image if

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.960
you're making a mobile video. Then

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.160
choose how many variations of the image

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.920
you want Nano Banana to make. Chances

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.880
are you'll want to pick four to give

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.800
yourself more choices. Then choose which

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.760
image generator you want to use. You'll

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.680
want to choose which image generator you

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.600
want. Right now, I slightly prefer Nano

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.160
Banana Pro, so I chose that. Then just

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.080
come and type in whatever you want your

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.760
starting frame to be. So, I decided to

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.960
generate a picture of a superhero

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.560
floating in space because I do follow

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.760
the Marvel Universe movies and TV shows

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.600
and I let Nano Banana do its magic. You

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:45.280
can see it generated four images to pick

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.440
from, but of course, if I want more, I

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.640
can hover over any of these images and

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.680
click the reuse prompt button and then

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.680
just generate more images. I think it's

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.400
definitely worth it to generate lots of

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.040
images to choose from so that you can

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.960
pick the one that suits the look and

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.280
aesthetic that you want your video to

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.440
look like. So, I did this a bunch times

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.840
and I ended up having a lot of different

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.080
choices to pick from, although some were

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.360
absolutely more photorealistic than

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.040
others. Like take this image here. This

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:15.040
one has a pretty realistic-looking face,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.720
whereas this one here, visually, it

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.400
looks really cool and would still make

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.000
for a cool video, but it definitely

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:24.160
looks less realistic. And some images

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.480
were just not so great, period. Like

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.840
take this one, weirdly, the helmet is

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.760
cracked. And in this one, our superhero

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:33.560
looks a lot more like than a guy. So,

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.760
yeah, I think it's good to give yourself

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.560
lots of choices. Either way, once you

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.560
have an image you like, you can edit it

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.560
too. So, for me, I wanted to replace

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.960
that crazy-looking space background with

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.920
something more realistic, just a dark

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.840
black space background with stars in the

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.680
distance. So, I asked Nano Banana to

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.600
make this change, which it did, no

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.840
problem. And then I also wanted it to

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.600
make that suit look more realistic, so I

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.800
explained to Nano Banana that it looked

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.160
too perfect and I wanted it to add more

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:03.000
texture to it along with imperfections,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.440
which again it added just fine. So then,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.360
once you got your image looking the way

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.600
that you want, just click download and

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.520
export it and upscale it. And for

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.760
realistic videos, you can choose either

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:15.640
2K or 4K, both work fine for our needs.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.520
And by the way, here are some advanced

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:19.760
Nano Banana prompting tips used by

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.560
advanced AI video creators to get the

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.000
best starting frames. So, the first tip

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.080
is that you can use Nano Banana's

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.080
editing tools to specify specific

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.160
changes. So, I used the selection tool

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.240
to highlight this light on our superhero

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.040
suit and I asked Nano Banana to remove

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.920
it, which it did. Then I used the

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:38.160
selection tool to highlight a section of

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.040
the image and I asked Nano Banana to add

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.240
the planet Earth into the background,

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:44.120
which again it did. And then I switched

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.280
to the drawing tool and drew some dots

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:48.560
on the picture and I asked it to replace

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:50.600
the dots with stars and again, it did

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.760
this for me. And here is another very

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.280
helpful tip. So, if you ever want

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:56.600
inspiration to help you create your

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.600
scenes, a popular way to do that is to

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:01.040
create a free account on a different AI

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:02.560
image generation website Midjourney and

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:04.600
to browse through their free public

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.880
gallery. You can even do a search for

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.240
specific images too. So, let's say I

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.120
wanted some inspiration for generating a

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.200
Roman soldier. Well, I can search

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.440
directly for Roman soldiers within their

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.680
library and it's free to do this, by the

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.560
way. You don't even need a paid account.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.600
And it was super helpful because if you

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.320
find an image like I did here, you can

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.920
download it and then you can upload it

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.320
back into Nano Banana through Google

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:29.200
Flow and ask it to use this as a style

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:32.080
and aesthetic guide to generate a new

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:34.720
picture for you. So then, let's now turn

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.760
our AI images into videos using Google's

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.400
video generator Veo. So, back inside of

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:43.520
Flow, come and start a new project and

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.360
this time choose Veo from the list. Now,

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.920
you will want to use the latest version

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.800
of Veo, which right now as of me filming

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.920
is 3.1. However, as you can see, there

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.800
are multiple versions. First, there is

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.520
quality mode. This is the best model

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.560
that produces the highest quality clips

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:00.800
with the most accurate prompt adherence

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.400
and the best detailing. These right now

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.560
cost 100 credits. Whereas the fast mode

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.320
still produces excellent clips that

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.840
aren't quite as good, but they are

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:12.480
significantly cheaper. On Google's pro

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.880
AI plan, right now these cost just 20

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:17.480
credits per clip. Or like me, if you

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.600
have an account on the ultra AI plan,

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:22.360
then you can generate Veo fast videos at

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:25.000
a slightly slower pace in the low

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:27.400
priority queue for free, which basically

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.880
means you can generate unlimited AI

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:31.560
videos. It's a crazy good deal, but on

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.880
the flip side, this is their most

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:36.520
expensive plan. So, whether being able

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:39.200
to generate unlimited AI videos is worth

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:40.560
it or not is up to you. Either way, once

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.000
you've chosen your model and how many

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.880
clips you want to generate, you'll be

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.520
able to choose between frame mode or

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.440
ingredients mode, but for the best

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:49.600
results, most people just use frame

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.360
mode. So, once selected, just upload the

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:53.680
starting image frame that we made with

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.760
Nano Banana. Then once it's uploaded,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.200
choose for it to be the starting frame.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.680
Then once you've done that, just type

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.320
into the prompt box what you want the

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.760
scene to show and how you want it to

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:06.000
play out and then just click submit and

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:08.480
let it do its AI magic. Now, obviously,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.040
I sped this up, but in total, even in

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.120
low priority mode that's slower, it took

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.680
just over 1 minute for it to generate

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:19.360
four different clips. So, here was my

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:19.360
favorite of the four clips.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.400
And for the sake of comparison, here is

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:34.280
the same video prompt again, but this

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:34.280
time made with quality mode turned on.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.080
And honestly, for this particular clip,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:45.840
I can't actually tell much of a

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.680
difference and in fact, in some ways for

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.280
this clip, I kind of prefer fast mode.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:51.840
So, I think a good idea is that if

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.920
you're creating something like say a

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.160
movie where you want to have the very

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:59.040
best clips possible, that you draft your

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.280
scenes and that you storyboard your

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:05.320
scenes and you test your prompts using

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:07.760
the fast mode of Veo on the ultra plan

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:10.080
for free and then you can use quality

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.000
mode after you've already used fast mode

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.120
to figure out your prompts and your

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.920
scenes and you can then choose between

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:17.880
these two which one gives you the best

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.560
clips. But on the other hand, if you're

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:22.840
just creating say a YouTube or Instagram

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.200
or Facebook or TikTok video, then I

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:27.680
reckon you're probably fine using just

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.040
fast mode most of the time because for

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.120
standard content videos, they don't have

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:34.080
to be perfect, you know? You can also

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.880
get characters to talk in your videos.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.160
So, remember earlier that podcast

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.880
between the two dogs? Well, I generated

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.960
that picture with Nano Banana and I

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:44.400
uploaded it into Flow and explained in

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:47.600
my prompt that they were podcasters

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:49.480
having a conversation and I specified

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:52.000
what they were saying to each other and

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:55.800
check out what it made. So, here is a

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:59.360
question. Who has it harder, dogs or

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:01.840
humans? Dogs, definitely dogs. It was

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:03.480
perfect. I loved it. Although, I did try

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.840
it again a few times to see what would

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.920
happen out of curiosity. And for some

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:09.600
clips, it really did struggle. Like

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:11.640
sometimes it would just completely

