higher resolution, then VEED will

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:05.800
upscale it. And while this might change

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:09.360
in the future, currently it is bad at

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:12.200
upscaling clips. Like really bad. So, I

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.240
downloaded this video here in 720p. It

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:16.440
isn't super sharp, but it looks normal,

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.280
right? Well, here's what happens when I

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:21.920
get VEED to upscale it for me. If you

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:24.839
look at his shirt, the detailing on it

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:26.160
looks a little bit weird and shiny. So,

00:11:24.839 --> 00:11:28.160
I recommend that you just stick to the

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.800
original resolution. And then if you do

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:32.720
want to upscale it, you can use a

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:35.520
dedicated AI upscaler to upscale it into

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.280
high resolution. And right now Topaz AI

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.440
is the overall best in the industry for

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:42.000
this and it's what I personally use for

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:44.320
my AI videos. So, for example, I popped

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.400
this clip into my Topaz video upscaler

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.960
app that I have installed on my computer

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:51.320
and I set the resolution to be two times

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:53.680
and then I exported it. And now check it

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:55.720
out. It looks nice, crisp, and sharp

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.720
without that shininess. And if we put

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:59.680
both videos side by side, I think you'll

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:02.120
see what I mean about how the Google

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:04.640
upscaled version just looks oddly shiny

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:07.280
with the clothing details. But do keep

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:09.440
in mind, upscaling clips is optional.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:13.040
I've seen lots of creators honestly get

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:15.600
massive traction by just using 720p

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.400
resolution AI clips. Especially

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:19.320
short-form creators on platforms like

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:21.400
TikTok, Facebook, and YouTube Shorts.

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:23.640
So, depending on how you plan to use

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:26.000
your videos, you can just keep them as

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:28.000
is. But either way, now that we know how

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:30.480
to generate videos, let's discuss some

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.040
advanced generation options that top AI

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.120
creators are using to get the best

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:37.120
results. So, here is something pretty

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:39.000
cool. If you open up a video that you

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:41.360
have generated, you'll get access to

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:43.280
advanced editing tools. For example, you

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:45.280
can click the camera icon and choose

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:47.720
from options. So, one thing you can do

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:49.839
is change the camera position or motion.

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:51.560
So, I selected to have it orbit low

00:12:49.839 --> 00:12:54.080
instead of doing its current panning

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:56.600
motion. And when it's done, it'll show

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.200
up in your generation history. So, check

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:00.000
it out, right? This clip here that I'm

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:01.880
playing, this was the original clip that

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.080
I started with. A different version of

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:06.480
that Roman soldier video from before.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:08.680
And then here is the new version, this

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:10.600
time with the orbit set to be low. The

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:12.960
animation stays the same, it's just the

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:14.240
camera motion that has now changed. And

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.880
there is something else that you can do

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:17.560
that I want to show you too. And that is

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:19.720
that you can insert or remove something

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:22.640
in the video. So, for me, I used the

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:24.520
insert button to get VEED to add a

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:26.760
legion of soldiers into the background

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:28.920
of the clip. And check it out, it added

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:31.120
them in off to the right of the clip and

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:33.400
they're even marching, which is very

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:35.480
cool. And there is another feature you

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.800
should know about and that is the extend

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.240
feature. With this, you will be able to

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.200
explain to the AI how you want to extend

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.680
the scene and then it will generate a

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:46.320
new 8-second clip for you that follows

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:48.120
on directly from your first clip. But I

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.280
will be honest, this feature is not

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:51.839
currently amazing. Like the first 8

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:54.160
seconds it made for me in the

00:13:51.839 --> 00:13:56.240
frame-to-video mode was fantastic and

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:58.600
looked pretty realistic. But then it

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:00.720
generated the new 8-second clip from

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:02.920
scratch. And not only was the transition

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.480
not very smooth, but it just kind of

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:07.360
looked a lot more like CGI. But it's

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:09.320
probably worth keeping an eye on this

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:11.760
because I reckon in the future as AI

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:13.040
video evolves, this feature might get a

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:15.000
lot better.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.400
So then, now that I've got my clips, I'm

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.560
going to show you how I edit them

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:20.680
together by using an AI-powered video

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:22.880
editor. And honestly, you can just use

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:25.640
any AI-powered video editor that you

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.400
have or want to use. Since I'm pretty

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:29.200
sure that all of them have these same

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:31.160
features that I'm going to show you now.

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:33.400
So, if you like the all-time classic

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:35.440
Adobe Premiere, you can use that. Or if

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:37.680
you like DaVinci Resolve, since after

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:39.880
all it is a free video editor, which is

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:41.560
an amazing bargain, you can use that

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:43.040
too. Personally though, I like to use

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:45.200
CapCut since I find it very

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:47.200
beginner-friendly to use. So, I will be

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:49.240
using this for my tutorial. They do have

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:50.920
a 30-day free trial on their website if

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:52.839
you want to try it out and follow along

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:54.760
with me. But either way, start a new

00:14:52.839 --> 00:14:57.280
video project in your video editor of

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:59.800
choice and upload any videos that you

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:01.680
want to edit. So, first up, I'll show

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:04.920
you how you can remove the music from an

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:06.800
AI video and to isolate the voice. And

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.760
this is important to know because, for

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:10.560
example, let's say that I have this clip

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:13.920
here with my superhero character

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:18.000
talking, but I don't like the music that

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:18.000
VEED edited into the scene.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:24.160
Oh, no.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:26.600
What are we going to do now?

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:28.600
Well, what I can do is add this clip to

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:30.959
my video editing timeline and then go to

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:33.160
the audio settings. There are a bunch of

00:15:30.959 --> 00:15:35.880
AI features that you can do to edit or

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:38.040
enhance the audio, but for this, we will

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:40.440
want to click the speech isolator and

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:42.880
then choose to isolate the speech. And

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:46.560
so, here is our new clip, this time

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:46.560
without the background sounds.

00:15:47.959 --> 00:15:52.839
Oh, no.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:54.480
What are we going to do now?

00:15:52.839 --> 00:15:56.240
Plus another editing tip that you

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:58.320
probably will want to know is how to

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:00.520
trim videos to get rid of any awkward

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:02.760
bits. Because as you may have noticed, a

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:04.360
lot of AI clips will have some sort of

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:07.040
weird awkwardness at either the

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:09.320
beginning or the end or both that you

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:11.760
probably want to get rid of. For

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:14.200
example, I created another clip of my AI

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:16.520
dog podcast and while the parts where

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:19.200
the dog is speaking were good, the rest

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:21.320
of it I needed to cut, especially the

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:23.640
ending. Like check it out and you will

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:26.800
see what I mean.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:30.720
No one makes them do a trick to earn it

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:30.720
first, you know? Mhm.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:35.480
So, editing out any dead air or awkward

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:38.120
bits is easy. Just add a clip to your

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:40.440
video editing timeline, then zoom in on

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:42.440
it, and with your cursor, find the edge

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:44.600
of the bit that you want to cut out and

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:47.040
place your timeline marker there. Then

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:49.120
click the split button, then select the

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:51.240
awkward bit of the clip, and click the

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:53.200
delete button. And now it's gone. So,

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:54.959
just go ahead and do that for any other

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:57.079
bits that you want to remove. So, for

00:16:54.959 --> 00:16:59.520
example, I removed a section of my clip

00:16:57.079 --> 00:17:02.720
at the end too. And so now, check it

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:05.280
out. No one makes them do a trick to

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:07.320
earn it first, you know? Very cool,

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:09.040
right? However, I am not done quite yet

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:11.480
because I also wanted to change the

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:13.760
dog's voice. And by far the most popular

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:16.199
way to do this right now is to use 11

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:17.959
Labs. And here is how you do it. First,

00:17:16.199 --> 00:17:20.600
with your clips on your video timeline,

00:17:17.959 --> 00:17:23.600
come and click export. Then uncheck the

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:26.560
video export and make sure that audio

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:29.800
export is selected and then export the

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:31.760
video as an MP3. Then come to 11 Labs.

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:33.679
This is the most popular AI voice

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:36.200
generator currently. You will need a

00:17:33.679 --> 00:17:39.280
subscription to use it and for me, their

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:41.760
lowest cost $5 plan was all I needed, so

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:43.679
that is what I got. Then once logged in,

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:46.160
click the voice changer tool and then

00:17:43.679 --> 00:17:48.240
upload your MP3 clip into it and then

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.200
click voices. So, for me, I filtered the

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.040
results to show English-speaking voices

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:54.400
that are male and then I typed into the

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:56.040
search that I wanted a podcast voice.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.880
Then I listened to the voices to find

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:59.600
the one that I liked. And so then after

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:01.480
I chose it, with the default settings

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:03.960
turned on for it, I clicked to generate

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.840
the audio and once it was finished, I

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:08.120
then downloaded this clip and I came

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:09.920
back to CapCut and I right-clicked my

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:12.360
clip with the dog and I selected to

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:14.560
separate the audio from the visuals and

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:16.640
then I selected the audio and I clicked

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.880
to delete it. And then I uploaded the

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:21.080
new audio file of the presenter speaking

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:24.240
from 11 Labs and I placed it in the

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:26.560
video timeline under the dog video. And

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:29.919
so now, here is the new clip featuring

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:33.120
the dog's new voice. No one makes them

00:18:29.919 --> 00:18:34.600
do a trick to earn it first, you know?

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:36.919
And this can be super handy for two

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:39.120
reasons. Firstly, sometimes you just

00:18:36.919 --> 00:18:41.240
don't like the AI voice that VEED

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:43.120
chooses for your character and you want

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:45.280
to change it. But secondly, if you're

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:47.159
combining clips together, chances are

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:49.320
the characters' voices are going to be

00:18:47.159 --> 00:18:51.520
different from clip to clip because

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:54.159
they're separate generations and so you

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:56.360
want to have a consistent voice across

00:18:54.159 --> 00:18:58.000
all of your clips. And so, here is a

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:00.280
completed video timeline of that

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:01.760
30-second podcast clip that I showed you

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:04.800
at the start of this video that had

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:07.080
consistent voices for each dog. I did

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:09.080
that by isolating each dog's voice and

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:10.760
then changing it in 11 Labs. And so now

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:12.600
I have a completed video that I can

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:15.000
download and upload to Facebook,

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:17.040
Instagram, TikTok, or YouTube. And

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:18.800
downloading it is actually quite simple.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:21.320
Just click export and give your clip a

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:24.200
name and this time turn both the video

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:26.040
and audio option on and you're done. So

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:28.280
then, if you'd like to learn even more

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:30.159
nano banana tricks that I personally

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:32.080
use, be sure to watch my next video here

00:19:30.159 --> 00:19:34.600
on screen and I will see you in the next

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.600
video.

